.TH CORMANI 1 "26 August 2026" "corMani 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cormani \- correspondence client for mail, calendar and messaging sites
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cormani
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B corMani
is a desktop correspondence client. It handles many mail accounts over IMAP and
SMTP, with calendars alongside them, and tracks which conversations are owed a
reply and which carry a deadline.
.PP
Messaging services that publish no interface for personal conversations appear
as panels in the same window. Their contents are not searchable and
.B corMani
does not pretend otherwise.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report the state of this installation \(em interpreter, toolkit, keyring,
store, calendars and tracked conversations \(em then exit. Requires no
display, and is the first thing to run when something is wrong.
.TP
.BI \-\-add\-account " ADDRESS"
Add one account. Asks for an app password, or opens a browser for OAuth,
connects to the server, and writes the account only once the credential has
been accepted \(em so a failed attempt leaves nothing behind. Use
.B \-\-provider
with anything but a well-known consumer address: a custom domain says nothing
about who hosts it.
.IP
The window offers the same thing at
.B File \(-> Add mail account
and it is the same code on a worker thread, so the two cannot disagree about
what adding an account means. The command line remains the right place for a
FIRST import, which runs for hours and survives the window being closed.
.TP
.BI \-\-provider " google|microsoft|imap"
Which provider an address belongs to. Inferred for consumer domains only.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " oauth2|password"
How to authenticate. Defaults to OAuth where the provider offers it. Google
still accepts an app password for mail; Microsoft accepts none.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " TEXT"
The display name to send with mail from this account. Defaults to the part of
the address before the at sign.
.TP
.BI \-\-imap\-host " HOST"
.TQ
.BI \-\-imap\-port " PORT"
.TQ
.BI \-\-smtp\-host " HOST"
.TQ
.BI \-\-smtp\-port " PORT"
Where to reach a server that is not a known provider's, for
.BI \-\-provider " imap"
\(em a university, a hosting company, a mail server of your own. Known
providers set all four from
.IR auth/providers.py ,
so these are needed only when nothing else knows the answer.
.TP
.BI \-\-resync " ADDRESS"
Discard one account's cached messages so that the next
.B \-\-sync
fetches them again. A sync is incremental by design and never revisits a
message once the folder's UIDNEXT has moved past it, which is right almost
always and wrong in one case: a parser fixed after the message was stored.
Refused while the account has changes not yet sent to the server.
.TP
.BI \-\-set\-oauth " PROVIDER"
Record this installation's own OAuth client id and secret. One registration
covers every account on that provider, and it is held in the system keyring.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-thunderbird " [PATH]"
Copy mail from a Thunderbird profile into a corMani account. The source is
opened read\-only \(em Thunderbird may stay running. With no PATH, looks under
.IR ~/.thunderbird .
Requires
.BR \-\-into .
Junk and Trash are skipped unless
.B \-\-include\-junk
is given. Incremental: a second run only reads bytes appended since the last.
.TP
.BI \-\-into " ADDRESS"
With
.BR \-\-import\-thunderbird ,
the configured account that receives the imported mail. Required, because
Thunderbird's on\-disk account names are server directories rather than
addresses.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-junk
With
.BR \-\-import\-thunderbird ,
also import Junk and Trash folders.
.TP
.BR \-\-sync " [" all | mail | calendars ]
Fetch once and exit, without opening a window \(em mail and calendars both,
which is what
.B F5
does in the window. Every account that is due is contacted in turn, changes
made offline are sent first, and what the server holds is stored. A first
import of a large account takes hours and is deliberately resumable: each pass
takes a bounded number of messages and says how many remain, so running this
repeatedly finishes the job. Name one half to run only that half \(em useful
because the mail import takes hours and the calendars take seconds. Requires no
display.
.TP
.BI \-\-calendars " [ADDRESS]"
Report every calendar this store knows about and exit: what each holds, which
window of dates has been fetched, whether the next pass will ask only what
changed or fetch the whole window again, and which are parked and why. The next
fortnight's events are listed under it, with all-day events marked as such.
Read-only and offline, like
.BR \-\-check ,
and never prints a sync token's value. Name an address to narrow it to one
account.
.TP
.BI \-\-filters " [ADDRESS]"
Report every filter rule and exit, in the order they run: what each one looks
at, what it does, whether it stops the run, and how many times it has matched
anything. Read-only and offline, like
.BR \-\-check ,
and it changes nothing \(em running the rules again over mail already stored is
done from the interface, where the result can be seen and taken back. Name an
address to narrow it to the rules that run against that account, which includes
every rule not tied to one account at all.
.PP
The match counts are the reason this exists. A filter is invisible when it works
and invisible when it does not, so a rule that stopped matching \(em because a
correspondent changed their address, or a folder was renamed \(em looks exactly
like a rule that had nothing to match. The count and the date of the last match
are the only evidence, and rules that have never matched anything are named
here and counted by
.BR \-\-check .
.TP
.B \-\-searches
Report every saved search and exit: what each one asks for, whether it is drawn
in the rail or kept in the Search menu only, and how much mail it holds right
now. Read-only and offline, like
.BR \-\-check .
No address is taken, because a saved search is not scoped to an account \(em its
own scope may name one, but that is part of what the search asks rather than a
way of narrowing this list.
.PP
One of the three numbers is the reason this exists, and it is the last. A saved
search whose folder was deleted, whose account was removed or whose tag was
dropped still draws in the rail, and draws as empty \(em which is
indistinguishable from a search that simply matches nothing today. The rail
marks such a search and
.B Edit \(-> Manage saved searches
explains it, but both of those are inside the window; this is the one that works
when the window is what is wrong.
.PP
A search that cannot run is kept and never deleted on the user's behalf: the
folder comes back when the account is re\-added.
.TP
.BI \-\-contacts " [QUERY]"
Report the address book and exit: everybody in it, every way of reaching each
of them, and which cards look like the same person twice. Read-only and
offline, like
.BR \-\-check .
Give a QUERY to narrow it to one name, organisation or address \(em which also
turns on the per-contact mail counts, left out of the full list because each
one is a scan of the message table and two hundred of them is a report nobody
runs twice.
.PP
The reason this exists is that the address book is the one part of the store
that is edited by things which are not the address book. A delivery report
marks a handle bounced during a sync; putting somebody on a tracked thread
creates a card for them. So the question worth asking from a terminal is what
has happened to the contacts while nobody was looking at them, and three of
these numbers answer it: the addresses that have bounced, the cards with no
address at all, and the possible duplicates.
.PP
A duplicate is the one that matters. Two cards for one person split their
handles between them, so the composer's bounce guard checks one and the
tracking layer files against the other, and neither is wrong in any way the
interface can show. They are named here and merged in
.BR "Tools \(-> Address book" ;
nothing is ever merged automatically, because a merge deletes a card and cannot
be undone.
.PP
.B \-\-check
also reports the tracking layer: how many conversations are being pursued, how
many are owed a reply, every hard deadline within a month named individually,
and how much has arrived that is on no thread. It reports the address book on
the same terms: how many people and handles, broken down by channel, and each
of the three states above that means something is wrong rather than merely so.
.PP
It reports the site panels as well: the version of the embedded browser and how
current its security patches are, which sites are turned on, and the directory
each site's session is kept in \(em which is what to delete to sign a site out
from outside the application. See
.B PANELS
below.
.TP
.B \-\-reindex
Build the derived data again from the messages already stored: the full-text
search index, and the conversations. Neither can be repaired in place \(em an
external-content index holds no copy of the text to check itself against, and a
conversation is an opinion about a chain of headers \(em so the honest answer
for derived data is to derive it again. Nothing is fetched and no message is
changed. Run it after a change to how either is built, or if search stops
finding something you can see.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-desktop
Install the desktop entry and the icon into
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME ,
so that
.B corMani
can be found in the applications menu and pinned. It also writes the icon
theme's
.I index.theme
and rebuilds the icon cache, and says whether that worked: a cache older than
the icon beside it is treated by GTK as authoritative, so an installation with
every file in the right place can still produce an application with no icon.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-desktop
Remove what
.B \-\-install\-desktop
wrote. The store, the configuration and the panel sessions are untouched.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
With
.BR \-\-sync ,
print only the per-account summary rather than a running commentary.
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Open a store of demonstration data instead of your mail, so the interface can
be looked at before any account is configured. Mail, contacts, calendars and
events over fifteen months \(em with the recurring ones expanded, so any week
of that range has something in it \(em and eight tracked conversations whose
timelines are filed from that mail by the real matchers, with the telephone
calls and notes a mailbox cannot hold. The demo store is a separate file in the
cache directory; your real store is not opened at all. Delete that file to
reset it.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cormani/cormani.toml
Optional preferences. Accounts are
.B not
configured here; they are added in the application and held in its database,
because the application edits them too.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/cormani/cormani.sqlite3
The message store. Losing it loses mail that has not been re-synchronised.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/cormani/web/
One directory per site panel, holding that site's cookies and its signed-in
session. DATA rather than cache: deleting one signs that site out. They are
kept apart from one another, so no site can see another's.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/cormani/
Disposable. Safe to delete, and not worth backing up. The
.B \-\-demo
store lives here, which is why it can never be confused with the real one.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/cormani/cormani.log
The log.
.SH TRACKING
.B corMani
tracks conversations rather than only messages. A THREAD here is something you
are pursuing \(em with a state, a next action, a nudge cadence and possibly a
deadline \(em and it holds a timeline of everything that happened on it: mail,
and also the telephone calls, meetings and notes that a mailbox cannot contain.
Open it with
.B Ctrl+Shift+O
or from the Tracking menu; it opens in a tab, and the tab's title carries the
counts.
.PP
.B Owed
is a fact and not a guess: they answered last and you have not. A call logged
on the thread answers it, because it did. A note to yourself does not.
.PP
.B A deadline is not a nudge.
A nudge is a follow-up and defaults to the last thing that happened plus the
thread's cadence; a deadline is statutory and nothing satisfies it but the
thing being done. They are kept apart everywhere, including on a thread that is
blocked \(em which silences the nudge and never the deadline.
.PP
.B What could not be filed is shown, not dropped.
Mail that belongs to no thread waits in a queue beside the board, narrowed by
default to people you have written to and by a horizon, with the wider counts
shown so nothing is hidden. Filing one message files its whole exchange and
puts the correspondent on the thread, so the next reply arrives filed.
.SH PANELS
WhatsApp, LinkedIn, X and Facebook publish no interface for personal
conversations. Not restricted, not paid \(em absent. What exists is a web
application a person signs into, so
.B corMani
shows that web application in a panel and does nothing else with it: no
reverse-engineered protocol client, and no automation that reads or sends a
message. Both halves matter, because a protocol client gets the account banned
and the account is the thing being protected.
.PP
The one exception is the unread count, and it is deliberately the smallest one:
a single expression reads the number already in the page's title \(em the same
characters a person reads on a browser tab \(em so the rail can carry a badge.
It exists only while a panel is open. There is no background polling and no
hidden page, so a site nobody has opened has no count rather than a count of
nought.
.PP
Each site has its own profile: its own cookies, its own cache, its own signed-in
session, and no way to see another's. A panel holds no connection to the message
store, cannot reach it, and screen capture, WebRTC's local addresses, hyperlink
auditing and reading back from a canvas are turned off. JavaScript is on \(em
there is no site here without it \(em and that is the honest cost of the
feature.
.PP
The panels are optional and are meant to stay that way. The embedded browser
comes from the distribution rather than being bundled, so it ages between
releases and these sites may eventually refuse it; mail and calendars are
unaffected when that happens. Set
.I sites
in the configuration file to choose which appear, or to
.I "[\(dqnone\(dq]"
for none at all.
.PP
Sign a site out from the
.B Panels
menu, which deletes that site's stored session and cannot be undone. The same
thing can be done from outside the application by deleting the site's directory
under
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/cormani/web/ ;
.B \-\-check
names them.
.SH SECURITY
Credentials are held in the system keyring and never written to the
configuration file, the database, or the log.
.PP
Remote content in a message \(em images above all \(em is blocked until
requested, because loading it tells the sender the message was opened.
.PP
Mail is fetched over TLS with certificate verification and hostname checking
on. Downloading a message does not mark it read on the server.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
With
.BR \-\-check ,
the installation is not usable. With
.BR \-\-sync ,
at least one account failed \(em in either half, since a scheduled run asks one
question and must get one answer \(em so that it is noticed rather than
appearing to have worked. With
.BR \-\-calendars ", " \-\-filters ", " \-\-searches " or " \-\-contacts ,
there is no store, or it predates the feature being asked about. With
.BR \-\-add\-account ,
nothing was added. The preceding lines say why in every case.
.TP
.B 2
An argument was not understood. Note that Qt's own switches take a single dash
and are passed through; anything unrecognised with two dashes is refused rather
than ignored.
